nodecode-coder is a rewrite of perqin's perqin/hermes-plugin-coder:

  https://github.com/perqin/hermes-plugin-coder
  commit 98e023da379961702fb0ce0cf03b983176039321
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Perqin (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's terminal backend, as three tools a model calls —
coder_workspaces, coder_workspace and coder_exec. What it did is kept whole:
the workspace search by name (owner:me name:X), the start of a stopped
workspace through a workspace start build followed by polling of that build's
job, the wait for an agent whose status is connected and lifecycle_state is
ready, the PTY websocket at /api/v2/workspaceagents/{id}/pty with the command
in the query string and the reconnect id, the exit marker printed after the
command and parsed back out of the terminal output, the CRLF folding of PTY
output, the bounded capture with the omitted count in the middle, and the
validations the original made of its own settings — an https origin with no
credentials, path, query or fragment, a header-safe token, a forward_env list
of environment variable names with the session token never forwarded into the
shell. The Python (requests, websockets) is Common Lisp here: nlk:http for
Coder's REST API, the websocket-driver client nodecode already loads for the
PTY, two function seams (*http*, *pty-read*) a test rebinds, one section
declaration for the settings, and three nlk:define-contract declarations for
the tools' arguments.

Left out: the plugin's other half, which is a Harness shape rather than a
verb — it registered a TerminalEnvironmentProvider so that Hermes's own shell
tool ran inside the workspace, with the session snapshot of forwarded
environment variables, the per-call process handle and its interrupt, and the
remote snapshot cleanup. A cell has no terminal-environment seam to swap, so
the same capability is reached by naming the workspace in a tool call instead;
forwarded environment is delivered as exports on the one command rather than
as a snapshot kept for a session, the interrupt-by-Ctrl+C path is replaced by
the command's own timeout, and the reconnect-until-the-marker loop is one
socket read under a deadline.
